{"blocks":[["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1049","Emulation is used when guest and host CPU families differ (for example, x86 guest on ARM host).",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Architecture coupling"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1150","Performance profile",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1178","Virtualization usually has lower overhead.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Performance profile"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1226","Emulation usually has higher overhead because of instruction translation.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Performance profile"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1305","Fidelity goals",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1328","Virtualization models an isolated machine boundary.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Fidelity goals"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1385","Emulation models hardware behavior and instruction semantics.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Key Principles","Fidelity goals"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1452","Mechanism",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1468","Virtualization",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1491","A hypervisor coordinates privileged operations, memory mapping, and device access while letting most CPU instructions run natively.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Virtualization"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1628","Hardware extensions (for example VT-x or ARM virtualization support) reduce trap-and-emulate overhead.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Virtualization"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1736","Emulation",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1754","An emulator decodes guest instructions and maps them into host operations (interpreter or dynamic binary translation).",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Emulation"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1878","Device models and firmware behavior are reproduced in software to match target expectations.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Mechanism","Emulation"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1976","Examples",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-1991","Running a Linux ARM64 VM on an Apple Silicon Mac with hardware acceleration is primarily virtualization.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2100","Running legacy x86 software on an ARM-only host without native x86 hardware support requires emulation.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2208","Hybrid systems (for example UTM via QEMU) may use virtualization when architectures match and emulation when they do not.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Examples"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2342","Misconceptions",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2363","Virtualization and emulation are the same thing -> False. They can look similar at the UI level, but the execution model is different.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2506","Emulation is always unacceptable for real work -> False. It can be essential for compatibility testing, reverse engineering, and legacy workloads.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2661","Virtualization always works for any guest OS on any host -> False. Cross-architecture scenarios generally require emulation.",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Misconceptions"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2794","Related",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2808","UTM",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2820","QEMU",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2833","Container",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["Programming/Concept/Virtualization%20vs%20Emulation/","source-block-2851","Apple/Hypervisor.framework",["Virtualization vs Emulation Conceptual Overview","Related"]],["Programming/Destructuring/","source-block-0","Destructuring",[]],["Programming/Destructuring/","source-block-18","Also known as: unpacking, destructuring assignment",["Destructuring"]],["Programming/Destructuring/","source-block-72","A programming concept that allows unpacking values from data structures (arrays, 